From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0000
Subject: drm/sun4i: de33: Latch the mixer and plane registers at vblank

The H616 DE33 is driven over plain MMIO: the blender and the plane
registers (overlay, scaler, CSC, AFBC decompressor) are written from the
atomic commit worker, through regmaps, at whatever point of the frame it
happens to run. Unlike DE2/DE3 there is no GLB_DBUFF: those engines
double-buffer every register and swap the set at the next vblank when the
driver sets the bit at the end of its commit. sun8i_mixer_commit() skips
that write for DE33, and the hardware's own mechanism, the RCQ DMA of the
register configuration queue (RCQ_CTL at top + 0x10, the same block the
A523 backend uses), is not used on this path.

So a DE33 register update is live the moment it is written. The DE33 AFBC
work measured where the commits land with afbc-kms-play on a forced
3840x2160@60 HDMI mode (no sink): a mean of 1.50 ms after the previous
vblank for a 4K XR24 pattern, with every one of 1499 commits inside the
active video (the blanking is 0.667 ms), and 6.1 ms mean for AFBC
playback. A page flip programmed at scan line ~1000 changes the fetch
address for the rest of that frame: the top of the frame is the old
buffer and the bottom the new one. A plane enable or disable programmed
there flashes the background colour for part of a frame. The sun55iw3
(A523) backend in this tree saw exactly that on hardware when it armed its
RCQ load from atomic_flush, and moved the arm into the vblank interrupt.

The vendor driver does the same for this engine. Its AHB path (RTMX_USE_RCQ
off) writes the dirty register blocks from the vsync interrupt
(sync_event_proc -> disp_sync_all -> disp_al_manager_sync ->
de_rtmx_update_reg_ahb), and its RCQ path issues the RCQ load from the
vsync interrupt as well (disp_mgr_protect_reg_for_rcq).

Do that here. Add a register shadow (sun50i_shadow.c): the DE33 mixer and
planes regmaps become bus-less regmaps whose writes land in a staging copy
(4 KiB pages, allocated for the register pages actually used). At the end
of the mixer's atomic flush, sun8i_mixer_commit() snapshots the staged
registers of this display (the blender block and the channels mapped to
it; the planes device is shared by both mixers) into a latch copy and takes
a vblank reference, so the TCON vblank interrupt stays enabled also for
commits without a page-flip event (legacy cursor updates). The TCON vblank
interrupt then writes the latched registers out through the engine's
vblank_quirk, while the beam is in the leading vertical blanking, and
drops the reference. The snapshot gives the same frame boundary as
GLB_DBUFF: a commit that starts before the previous one's vblank is not
mixed into it. While the CRTC is off, drm_crtc_vblank_get() fails and the
registers are written at once: nothing scans out, and the planes of a
modeset are programmed before the CRTC is enabled.

Read-only diagnostics come as module parameters
(/sys/module/sun8i_mixer/parameters/de33_latch_*): flush count, flushes
written with the CRTC off, flushes where the interrupt found the beam in
the active video (late), the TCON line at the flush (min/max) against the
first active line of the mode, the biggest flush in registers and ns, and
commits that coalesced. A frame-coherent update needs late == 0 and
line_max < line_active. The TCON TV line counter is taken to be zeroed at
the start of the vertical sync, as measured on the sun55iw3 TCON TV; a
flush that the interrupt services late is still written immediately
(counted), which is what happens on every commit today.

The DE2/DE3, A523 RCQ and TVE paths are unchanged: the shadow is only
created for a DE33 mixer without uses_rcq, and only the "display" and
"planes" regmaps go through it; the "top" block (RT_EN, size, clock) stays
plain MMIO and is written at mode set with the CRTC off.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
